  "I1" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Conclusion: Given pathogens are a challenge and evade antibiotics, therefore resistance is a widespread mechanism."];
  "I11" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-Conclusion: Given peptides induce aggregation and mode is based on APRs, therefore APRs drive aggregation."];
  "I12" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-Conclusion: Given proteins have APRs and APRs are in low mutation sites, therefore APR targeting is stable."];
  "I14" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Conclusion: Given peptides target beta-lactamases and differentiate classes, therefore targeting is specific."];
  "I18" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-Conclusion: Given disruption explored and P33 turned to, therefore P33 is the candidate for disruption."];
  "I19" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Conclusion: Given screen selected APRs and P33 is a hit, therefore P33 is an APR-based peptide."];
  "I2" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Conclusion: Given specific pathogens cause millions of deaths and form the ESKAPE group, therefore ESKAPE is a major burden."];
  "I22" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-Conclusion: Given profile seen in classical AMPs and lack of resistance observed, therefore P33 acts like AMP but with low resistance."];
  "I28" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Conclusion: Given study focuses on P33 and broad-spectrum activity found, therefore P33 has broad-spectrum potential."];
  "I5" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Conclusion: Given AMPs are explored for ESKAPE and are innate immune components, therefore AMPs are natural candidates."];
  "I6" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Conclusion: Given AMPs are developed for activity and have variable sequences, therefore synthetic design is feasible."];
  "I8" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-Conclusion: Given initially membrane disruption and now diverse mechanisms, therefore AMP mechanism understanding is evolving."];
  "I9" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-Conclusion: Given AMP mechanism is evolving and amyloid formation exists, therefore amyloid-like action is a potential mechanism."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) The paper proposes synthetic peptide P33, built from tandem repeats of an aggregation-prone transmembrane-region motif and enriched with arginines, as a broad-spectrum antibacterial strategy against ESKAPE pathogens by exploiting bacterial protein aggregation, including membrane-protein and ribosomal-protein aggregation, to disrupt bacterial proteostasis.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) Common bridge pattern: a synthetically designed, cationic APR-based peptide can be evaluated as an antibacterial strategy when its design enables bacterial uptake, its mechanism is linked to protein aggregation, and its activity spans ESKAPE pathogens."];
  "R24" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Conclusion: P33 follows a design pattern with a tandem repeat of an aggregation-prone region flanked by arginines, and the arginine residues support potential bacterial uptake."];
  "R30" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-Conclusion: P33 has an aggregation-dependent mechanism, and amyloid-indicative inclusion-body spectra support amyloid-like protein aggregation as an associated mechanistic feature."];
  "S1" [label="(1,0,0) Case: Drug-resistant pathogens have emerged as a major global health challenge in recent decades."];
  "S10" [label="(10,0,0) Phenomenon: Initially, AMPs were regarded as molecules exerting effects by disrupting bacterial membranes."];
  "S11" [label="(11,0,0) Knowledge: Mechanism of action of AMPs is now recognized as more diverse and can involve intracellular targets."];
  "S12" [label="(12,0,0) Phenomenon: A subclass of AMPs has been shown to undergo amyloid formation."];
  "S13" [label="(13,0,0) Case: Researchers recently designed antimicrobial peptides with a novel mechanism inducing aggregation."];
  "S14" [label="(14,0,0) Commonality: The mode of action is based on aggregation-prone regions (APRs) within polypeptide sequence."];
  "S15" [label="(15,0,0) Case: Most proteins have at least one APR in their sequence."];
  "S16" [label="(16,0,0) Commonality: APRs are typically located in hydrophobic core or transmembrane regions where mutation rates are low."];
  "S17" [label="(17,0,0) Case: Peptides designed to target beta-lactamases exhibit a knock-out phenotype."];
  "S18" [label="(18,0,0) Commonality: These peptides can differentiate between related classes of beta-lactamases."];
  "S2" [label="(2,0,0) Commonality: These bacteria evade antibiotics through various multi-drug resistance mechanisms."];
  "S21" [label="(21,0,0) Case: Researchers explored whether disruption of membrane protein folding could yield broad-spectrum activity."];
  "S22" [label="(22,0,0) Commonality: For this purpose, we turned to a peptide called P33 previously identified in a screen."];
  "S23" [label="(23,0,0) Case: The screen was designed to select APRs with maximal homologous fragments across E. coli proteome."];
  "S24" [label="(24,0,0) Commonality: P33 emerged as a potent hit based on an APR from transmembrane region of RhtA."];
  "S25" [label="(25,0,0) Case: The peptide follows a typical design pattern with tandem repeat of APR flanked by arginine."];
  "S26" [label="(26,0,0) Commonality: Arginine residues are known to be critical for bacterial cell uptake."];
  "S27" [label="(27,0,0) Case: This profile of hydrophobic peptides with positive charge is seen in classical AMPs."];
  "S28" [label="(28,0,0) Commonality: A lack of resistance build-up against this peptide was observed in E. coli."];
  "S29" [label="(29,0,0) Phenomenon: The mechanism of action of P33 is dependent on protein aggregation."];
  "S3" [label="(3,0,0) Case: Six pathogens including E. coli and S. aureus were linked to 3.57 million deaths in 2019."];
  "S38" [label="(38,0,0) Phenomenon: The inclusion body spectra exhibit a shoulder at 1630 cm-1, indicative of amyloid formation."];
  "S4" [label="(4,0,0) Commonality: ESKAPE refers to a group of highly virulent and antibiotic-resistant bacterial pathogens."];
  "S44" [label="(44,0,0) Deduction-Conclusion: Given P33 solution is supported and if supported then study focused, therefore study focuses on P33 mode and activity."];
  "S45" [label="(45,0,0) Case: We screened for activity against other ESKAPE pathogens and found broad-spectrum activity."];
  "S6" [label="(6,0,0) Case: Antimicrobial peptides (AMPs) are increasingly being explored to combat ESKAPE pathogens."];
  "S7" [label="(7,0,0) Commonality: Naturally occurring AMPs are integral components of the innate immune system of many species."];
  "S8" [label="(8,0,0) Case: Researchers have developed both natural and synthetic AMPs valued for fast bactericidal activity."];
  "S9" [label="(9,0,0) Commonality: AMPs are typically short, cationic, amphipathic peptides with variable sequences."];
